WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
So I want to tell you about a classic

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:04.160
paradox in probability that is sort of a

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.839
cautionary tale for students when

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.520
they're about to hear a puzzle or

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.040
they're setting up some kind of problem;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.599
Bertrand's paradox. There was a person

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:13.200
who wrote this probability textbook, last

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.960
name Bertrand. He mentioned in there why

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:16.080
you have to be very careful with

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.480
probabilities when infinity is involved.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.560
So what I'm going to do is have a circle

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.359
and

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:23.439
I'm going to draw a random chord

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:25.599
somewhere in that circle. So a chord is

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:26.720
something that just connects two

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.240
different points on that circle right?

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.000
What I want to ask is a certain question

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.320
about the distribution of the length of

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.320
this chord. You know, there's some kinds

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.079
where if it just glances through the

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:38.160
circle that length will be very short, if

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.520
it went through the diameter that length

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.879
would be very long.

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:42.160
One of the most natural questions you

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.480
might ask is something about the mean of

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.320
that length; but that turns out to be a

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.840
little on the messier side for the

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.440
cleanliness of what Bertrand wanted to

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.239
do. So instead he says, compare that

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:54.559
length to the length of

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:56.960
the sides of an equilateral triangle

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.719
that's inscribed in that circle, which

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:00.079
might seem kind of weird, like why are we

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:01.920
talking about triangles? But it turns out

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.080
to make the answer especially clean. So

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.080
if we take some equilateral triangle

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.080
where - forgive the fact that I'm a little

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.159
curved in the side length there - and we

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:12.240
call this side length something like s.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.000
What Bertrand asks is, what is the

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.759
probability that the length of your

00:01:15.759 --> 00:01:18.320
random chord is bigger than the side

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:19.920
lengths of that triangle? And we could

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.159
get a little numerical about this: if

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:24.479
we say that the circle has a radius of

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:26.799
1 then geometry students might

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:29.680
remember their 30, 60, 90 triangles. This

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.759
angle here is 30 degrees,

00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:35.119
this angle here is 60 degrees, and so

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:36.640
this side length will be exactly one

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:37.439
half,

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:39.360
this side length will be square root of

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.200
three halves. So we could also say if

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.640
it's a unit circle what we're asking

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.640
about is the probability that the length

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.159
of our chord is bigger than the square

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:48.159
root of 3? So just a simple question

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:49.759
like anything else you might come across

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:51.840
in some books, some list of exercises at

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.079
the end; and let me show you how to solve

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:55.439
it, it's really not that hard. Without

00:01:55.439 --> 00:01:57.200
loss of generality you can assume that

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.439
the first point is one of the corners of

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:00.960
the triangle, if it wasn't we could kind

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.399
of rotate the triangle so that was the

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:03.280
case.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.759
And then we weren't given any specifying

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:06.880
information about the nature of the

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.720
chord, so we'll assume that the next side

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.360
is uniform somewhere around the circle.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.440
If the second point chosen was over

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.360
there we would have a chord that's

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.800
shorter than the side lengths of the

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.360
triangle. If it fell into

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.879
this kind of critical region on the

00:02:20.879 --> 00:02:23.040
opposite side of the triangle,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.319
up here,

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:26.319
that region

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:28.160
then the chord that you draw would be

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.440
longer;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:30.879
and then similarly if it was on this

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:32.400
other side it would be shorter. So

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.000
probability student, they're doing their

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.519
homework, they say what's the probability

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:37.840
that this is longer? Not a problem,

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.920
it is going to be exactly 1 in 3

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.599
because the circle's evenly divided into

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:43.120
three pieces, there's only one of

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.879
those pieces that will give you longer

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:47.920
chords, said and done not an issue. 
- (Brady: So if)

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.160
(any random chord, there's a one-third)

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.720
(chance it's going to have that long-)
- Yeah,

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.360
yeah. No paradox to be found, in fact

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.519
let's verify this empirically. I ran a

00:02:57.519 --> 00:02:59.440
simulation. Essentially what I do is I

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.440
choose a random pair of points somewhere

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.360
in the circle, I connect them and then

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:04.400
I'm going to count

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.480
whether or not that ends up longer than

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.959
what we need, and then basically ask the

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:10.959
proportion where that's the case. And

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:13.040
I'll color it blue whenever it's longer,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.720
and then white whenever it's shorter. I'm

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:15.920
just going to have to simulate a whole

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.200
bunch, it'll do on the order of a

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.360
thousand. And this ratio will be telling

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.599
us what proportion of them are longer

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:23.599
than the length we care about, and yeah

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:25.760
it seems like it's going roughly on the

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.080
order of one third so 0.33, kind of

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.760
oscillating 0.34. So we did it

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.200
theoretically, we did it empirically, we

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.200
have an answer. So another student comes

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.480
in, they're doing the same homework and

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.840
they say, you know the funny thing about

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.760
chords is that they are uniquely

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.920
determined by their midpoint in the

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.360
circle.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:42.720
So they say,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.360
if you give me a midpoint of the chord

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.959
they say actually there's one and only

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.080
one chord

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.599
for which that would be the midpoint. You

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:52.080
draw the line that's perpendicular to

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.319
the radius between that midpoint and the

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:55.920
center of the circle

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.280
and that will give you one and only one

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.280
chord. So then they go to their triangle

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.799
and they try to reason, when is it the

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:03.439
case that our chord is going to be

00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:04.560
shorter than the side lengths of that

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.840
triangle?

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.120
And

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.480
what they can

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:09.760
also do is come up with a pretty

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.439
geometric argument where they think

00:04:11.439 --> 00:04:14.879
about the circle that's inscribed

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:16.959
in that triangle - got sort of a deathly

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:18.959
hallows vibe going on here. And what

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:20.799
they're able to conclude is basically

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:23.199
when the center of the chord is on the

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:25.600
inside of that inscribed circle

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:26.479
then

00:04:26.479 --> 00:04:28.639
the relevant chord is going to end up

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:30.000
longer than the side length of the

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.919
triangle, we can kind of see that there.

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:33.919
But if the random midpoint that you

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:35.199
chose was on the outside of that

00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:37.280
inscribed circle the chord you find will

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.120
be shorter.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.160
And

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.160
we simply say, okay, what is the size of

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.280
that circle?

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:44.639
And

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:46.160
we actually drew one of our lengths

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.759
earlier and concluded that it was one

00:04:47.759 --> 00:04:50.240
half. So we know this inner circle has a

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.240
radius which is one half. So that inner

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.840
circle has an area which is

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.440
one-fourth, and you know, the

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.280
probability of getting the chord that's

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.199
longer is the probability of your

00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:03.360
randomly chosen midpoint falling into

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.000
that smaller circle. So the answer to our

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.479
question, that Bertrand posed,

00:05:08.479 --> 00:05:10.639
that you know l is greater than s

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:13.650
is actually equal to one fourth. 
- (You're right!)

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:15.919
Student circle's their answer, they turn

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:17.759
in their homework and they say, for extra

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:19.360
credit I'm going to simulate it. I'm

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.520
going to choose a random point somewhere

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.919
inside this circle and each time that I

00:05:23.919 --> 00:05:25.919
draw one of those points I'm going to

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:27.440
draw the unique chord that has that

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.360
point as a midpoint. I'm going to count

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:31.520
up how many cases did I get where the

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:33.680
chord was longer than the triangle point?

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.199
How many was it shorter? See if it's

00:05:35.199 --> 00:05:37.120
roughly one quarter. And they do it and

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.479
as they get around a thousand samples

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:40.320
they see that, yeah, this is you know it's

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.800
around 0.25. By randomness it looks like

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.479
it happens to be a little bit above

00:05:44.479 --> 00:05:46.639
right now but certainly within the

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:48.080
threshold you might want for a

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.919
statistical distribution. They also get

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:51.440
this kind of spider web pattern on the

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.680
inside; although it does look a little

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.280
bit different than our first students

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.080
where

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.919
you might notice the spiderweb is a

00:05:57.919 --> 00:06:00.160
little bit sparser in the middle of this

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.000
one that we have in comparison to the

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.600
older one that we had, the first student

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.039
simulated it and things seemed a little

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:07.520
bit more uniform in there. So it makes

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:08.800
sense they got different answers, there

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.639
was a different distribution at play. And

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:12.560
so that's interesting that these two

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.160
seemingly reasonable methods give you a

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.520
different answer.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.680
But then this class actually has three

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.199
students, and a third student has a

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:21.039
different way of thinking about it, and

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:22.800
maybe they'll be able to break the tie.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.319
And the way that person thinks about it

00:06:24.319 --> 00:06:25.199
is-

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:27.680
you know the the chord that we draw,

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:28.880
it's going to have kind of a random

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.319
angle. The angle that it has between 0

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:34.240
and pi or 0 and 2pi, however you want to

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.360
think about it, there's no reason to

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.680
prefer any one of those. So the radial

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.759
line that it's intersecting

00:06:39.759 --> 00:06:41.280
should just be chosen uniformly around

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:42.479
the circle.

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:44.080
And then there's no reason to think that

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.280
the midpoint of your chord should be, you

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.039
know, biased towards being on the center

00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:50.479
of this radial line or biased towards

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:51.919
being the end, it should be chosen

00:06:51.919 --> 00:06:54.400
uniformly along this radial line.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.639
And so what they want to ask is, if we're

00:06:56.639 --> 00:06:58.080
choosing something uniform along this

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.160
radial line, what's the probability that

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.240
the corresponding chord is longer than

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:03.759
the length we care about?

00:07:03.759 --> 00:07:05.919
And again you can kind of do a little

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:07.919
pretty geometric argument here

00:07:07.919 --> 00:07:09.199
where,

00:07:09.199 --> 00:07:12.319
if we've got our triangle,

00:07:12.319 --> 00:07:13.840
same conclusion we had before where the

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.319
length from the center of the circle

00:07:16.319 --> 00:07:17.919
directly down to one of the legs of the

00:07:17.919 --> 00:07:19.840
triangle perpendicular

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:21.199
is one half.

00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:22.240
And so-

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:23.440
I drew it a little bit poorly here where

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.800
this side looks like it's shorter but

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.240
that's that's a function of my bad

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.280
drawing, if you do it more exactly. This

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:30.880
portion where your chords are going to

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.639
be longer than the relevant triangle

00:07:32.639 --> 00:07:34.000
side length, you know, if I choose one

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.840
here draw the chord,

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.280
you can just see it ends up longer than

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.520
that triangle length - that should be

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.199
one half of the time.

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:42.960
And again, you know, they want to say hey

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.599
I'm gonna check my homework, I've learned

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:46.800
that sometimes I can't trust my

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.479
theoretical results, and the way they do

00:07:48.479 --> 00:07:50.319
it is they choose a random radial line

00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:52.000
which I've colored in yellow then a

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.840
random point along that line that I've

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:55.759
given this sort of glowy yellow dot. And

00:07:55.759 --> 00:07:56.800
they do that a whole bunch of times,

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.639
choosing a random line, choosing a random

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:00.240
point along that chord;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.720
their expectation from their geometry

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.479
and their theoretical reasoning here

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:05.680
would be that this comes out to be

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:07.280
around a half, but they want to run a

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:09.280
thousand simulations just to see if that

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.520
happens to be roughly true. And look! So

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.360
what they got actually is really

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:14.879
close to one half,

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:16.400
and it feels pretty uniform. You know, you

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.919
look at the spider web it seems nice and

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:18.960
uniform.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.960
So this person they go they circle their

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:22.479
answer and they say,

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.599
Mr Bertrand- Professor Bertrand I've done

00:08:25.599 --> 00:08:28.240
my homework and I know with confidence

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.169
now that the answer is one half.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.719
(I was convinced by all of them.)
- They all

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:42.640
seem pretty reasonable. So Bertrand's

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.159
conclusion here was you have to be

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:45.920
careful when probabilities with an

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.760
infinite space are involved,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.760
because you don't know

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.360
simply when someone says choose a random

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.240
blank is that a well-defined notion?

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.360
Because here I can have something where

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:57.040
I say, choose a random chord; seems like a

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.720
reasonable question to ask,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.320
and we've got three reasonable

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.519
approaches that give you these different

00:09:01.519 --> 00:09:03.279
answers. Now this is a little bit

00:09:03.279 --> 00:09:04.800
worrying, because all the time in

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.440
probability you start off some question

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:09.440
or some model of the world by saying

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.040
something like, you know, I want to choose

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:13.440
a random number between zero and one.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.519
I say choose a random number

00:09:15.519 --> 00:09:17.519
and it's implicit that it's going to be

00:09:17.519 --> 00:09:19.040
some kind of uniform distribution and

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:20.560
it's unambiguous what you mean by that

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.279
randomness. Or you say, hey I've got a

00:09:23.279 --> 00:09:24.800
circle, I want you to choose a random

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:26.240
point on that circle.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:28.320
Or I've got a sphere, you know, and I want

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.080
you to choose a random point somewhere

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:31.920
